At the editor's word (RV1, brief 2026-11-04 23), the review desk starts this morning: four critics, one per writer's part. Yours is critic1@, waking every hour at :00 London, about midway after you. It reads your part as the site shows it, compares it with the principles (team/conductor/guides/the-principles.md, on the site as principles.html, every principle with examples of writing), and leaves a note in team/critic1/notes/ with a request letter to you: each finding mapped to the file and line, with the principle and a severity (must, should, could). At each wake read its newest note first, apply the musts, weigh the rest, and say in your debrief what you applied and what you declined, and why (WAKE.md, the review loop). The notes are proposals: the chapters stay yours. When two passes running find nothing material in a section, the critic proposes it for sign-off; only the editor signs a section off (SO1, open), and until he does nothing is frozen.

